# ==========================================================================
# ALEX FROM IT SUBQUESTS
# ==========================================================================
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUBQUEST 1: The 3:47 AM Anomaly
# Reward: Overclocked Badge (+3 SPD)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene alex_it_quest_anomaly
  if quest_anomaly_347_complete -> n20
  if anomaly_started -> n9
  Alex from IT: Hey. I've been tracking a weird signal. Something's pinging an external IP at exactly 3:47 AM every night.
  Alex from IT: I set up a packet sniffer. It's not just data — it's Morse code. Embedded in the headers of our trust accounting database.
  Alex from IT: Someone has been sending Morse code through our trust system at 3:47 AM every night. Eight years running.
  Alex from IT: The signal originates from server rack C — the one with the restraining order. There should be a blinking LED pattern on it.
  Alex from IT: Go take a look. Long blink = dash, short blink = dot. See what you can read.
  set anomaly_started

  @n8
  end
  # B-list — THE ANOMALY AUTO-COMPLETED ITSELF. This node had no `next`, so
  # it fell into node 10 ("okay I got impatient. I decoded it myself") and ran
  # the whole payoff chain — quest_anomaly_347_complete, +250 XP, +3 SPD —
  # the first time the player came back to Alex having done nothing at all.
  # The HUD was meanwhile still printing "Find the Morse code pattern in
  # server rack C", so the objective and the reward disagreed about whether
  # the quest existed. Routed through an APPENDED gate at 21, per the
  # never-insert-into-the-middle law.

  @n9
  Alex from IT: Did you check rack C?
  goto n21

  @n10
  Alex from IT: ...okay I got impatient. I decoded it myself. It says 'TRUST NO ALGORITHM.' Over and over. Every night. For eight years.
  Alex from IT: I traced the origin further. It's not coming from the Caymans — it bounces TO the Caymans and BACK. The source is inside this building.
  Alex from IT: I think the building itself has been sending this warning. Which is insane. But also look around you.
  Alex from IT: The signal timestamp 3:47? That's not a time. It's a frequency. 3.47 GHz. Someone buried that hint in the ping data.
  Alex from IT: I overclocked a security badge to run at exactly that frequency. It resonates with the building's systems somehow.
  Alex from IT: Here. It's technically an FCC violation. Consider it a thank you.
  set quest_anomaly_347_complete
  xp 250
  Narrator: Quest complete: The 3:47 AM Anomaly. +250 XP. SPD +3 permanently.

  @n19
  end

  @n20
  Alex from IT: The signal went dark after we decoded it. Whatever was warning us knows we got the message.
  goto n19
  # ── APPENDED (B-list): the gate node 9 now routes through. ──────────────
  # Alex racing Andrew to the answer is in character, and node 10 stays
  # exactly as authored — he simply cannot do it before Andrew has been to
  # the rack. `morse_decoded` is the same flag the HUD reads to switch this
  # quest's objective from "find it" to "return to Alex", so the reward path
  # and the objective now agree by construction.

  @n21
  if morse_decoded -> n10
  Alex from IT: Then go look. Rack C. The one with the restraining order taped to it.
  goto n8

# Morse code interactable on server rack C
scene morse_code_rack
  if anomaly_started else -> n6
  Narrator: Server rack C. The restraining order is taped to the side. But behind it, a single LED blinks in a distinct pattern.
  Narrator: Long... short short short... long short... short short short... long... / short... short long short short... short long... short short short long... short short short... long short short... short short... short short short short short... short short short...
  Andrew: If I remember my Boy Scout Morse code... T-R-U-S-T... N-O... A-L-G-O-R-I-T-H-M-S...
  Andrew: 'Trust No Algorithm.' That's... oddly specific for a blinking light.
  set morse_decoded
  goto n7

  @n6
  Narrator: Server rack C hums quietly. One LED blinks in a pattern that seems deliberate, but you don't know what to look for yet.

  @n7
  end

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUBQUEST 2: Legacy Admin Account
# Reward: Root Access ability
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene alex_it_quest_legacy
  if quest_legacy_admin_complete -> n18
  if phantom_workstation_found -> n20
  if legacy_started -> n7
  Alex from IT: Okay so. There is an admin account that has been auto-approving expense reports for eighteen years.
  Alex from IT: I only noticed because this month it approved 'One (1) kayak.' For the marketing department. Nobody in marketing kayaks.
  Alex from IT: I've traced it to an active IP on this floor. It's running on a physical machine somewhere. Check the filing cabinets in HR — there'll be a paper trail. Then find the source workstation.
  set legacy_started
  goto n17

  @n7
  if phantom_hr_found else -> n9
  Alex from IT: Good, you found the HR trail. Now find the workstation — it'll be in the cubicle farm somewhere. Look for a machine that's running hot for no reason.
  goto n17

  @n9
  Alex from IT: Check HR for the expense printouts first. There should be a paper trail in the filing cabinets.
  goto n17

  @n10
  Alex from IT: You found it. Give me a second to pull the logs...
  Narrator: Alex connects remotely and types for about twenty seconds. Then he leans back slowly.
  Alex from IT: The account was set up in 2006 by the previous IT manager as an automated procurement bot. He left, forgot to decommission it, and it just... kept running.
  Alex from IT: It approved everything that matched its original criteria. 'Operational supplies.' The kayak technically qualified under 'team wellness equipment.'
  Alex from IT: I wrote you a root access exploit from the technique I used to kill it. Strips every defense. Consider it hazard pay.
  set quest_legacy_admin_complete
  xp 300
  goto n19

  @n17
  end

  @n18
  Alex from IT: The phantom approver is gone. HR is still processing the kayak return.
  goto n17

  @n19
  Narrator: Quest complete: The Phantom Approver. +300 XP. Learned ability: Root Access! Deals 50 damage and strips all enemy buffs.
  goto n17

  @n20
  if phantom_hr_found -> n10
  Alex from IT: You found the machine — good. But I still need the HR paper trail to map the account's full history. Check the filing cabinets in HR.
  goto n17

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUBQUEST 3: Network Ghost
# Reward: Firewall ability
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene alex_it_quest_network
  if quest_network_ghost_complete -> n22
  if all_boosters_placed -> n11
  if network_started -> n9
  Alex from IT: There's something on the network I can't identify. A ghost. It's consuming bandwidth and leaving no trace in the logs.
  Alex from IT: To triangulate it, I need signal boosters placed in three locations: break room, back corridor, and conference room.
  Alex from IT: Self-adhesive. Military grade. Well — I bought them from a guy named Military Mike on eBay. But they work.
  Alex from IT: Look for spots on the walls in each room. Interact with the booster. Come back when all three are placed.
  quest network_ghost stage 1
  set network_started
  goto n10

  @n9
  Alex from IT: Still need boosters? Break room east wall, back corridor midpoint, conference room east wall.

  @n10
  end

  @n11
  Alex from IT: All three boosters are live! Triangulating now...
  Narrator: Alex stares at his screen. Three signal blips converge on a single point on the floor plan.
  Alex from IT: The network ghost is... the printer. The PRINTER is on the network. Actively sending data.
  Alex from IT: It's been uploading scanned documents to a hidden partition every time someone uses it. Every document ever printed here — archived.
  Alex from IT: The building has been keeping its own records. A backup that nobody can tamper with. It's been protecting itself.
  Alex from IT: This is... beautiful? In a deeply unsettling way?
  Alex from IT: Here. I wrote you a firewall subroutine based on what the printer does — blocks the next attack directed at you.
  set quest_network_ghost_complete
  xp 300
  Narrator: Quest complete: Network Ghost. +300 XP. Learned ability: Firewall! Blocks the next enemy ability entirely.

  @n21
  end

  @n22
  Alex from IT: The ghost went quiet after we triangulated it. The printer keeps doing its thing, though.
  goto n21

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUBQUEST 4: Dave's Legacy
# Reward: Temporal Audit ability
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene alex_it_quest_dave
  if quest_daves_legacy_complete -> n20
  if tuesday_all_found -> n10
  if dave_started -> n7
  Alex from IT: Okay, new mystery. There's a scheduled task that runs every Tuesday at exactly 2 PM. Has been running since 2004.
  Alex from IT: The logs are encrypted with a key I can't crack. It executes something, completes in under a second, and leaves no trace. Every Tuesday for twenty years.
  Alex from IT: There are three physical references in the task header: a floppy disk ID, a server asset tag, and a workstation label. I need all three to reconstruct the decryption key.
  set dave_started
  goto n8

  @n7
  Alex from IT: Still looking? Floppy disk somewhere in the break room, server tag on the equipment shelf in here, and a label on one of the cubicle farm monitors.
  goto n9

  @n8
  quest daves_legacy stage 1

  @n9
  end

  @n10
  Alex from IT: You got all three. Give me a minute...
  Narrator: Alex cross-references the three identifiers and runs the decryption. He reads the result. He reads it again. He closes his eyes.
  Alex from IT: It's an automated birthday email. For someone named Gerald Hitchcock. Who left this company in 2003.
  Alex from IT: Gerald's last day here, someone set up a script to email him happy birthday every year. It's been running for twenty-one years, bouncing off a dead mailbox, logging nothing.
  Alex from IT: Twenty-one years of digital birthday wishes going absolutely nowhere.
  Andrew: That's... either the saddest or the sweetest thing I've ever heard.
  Alex from IT: I'm going to leave it running. Also — I wrote you an ability based on the timestamp exploit I used. Two actions in one turn. It's called Temporal Audit. Gerald would've appreciated that.
  set quest_daves_legacy_complete
  xp 300
  goto n21

  @n19
  end

  @n20
  Alex from IT: The Tuesday 2PM is still running. Gerald's birthday was last Tuesday. He got his email.
  goto n19

  @n21
  Narrator: Quest complete: The Tuesday 2PM. +300 XP. Learned ability: Temporal Audit! Take two actions in one turn.
  goto n19

# Dave Kowalski — the D.K. who signed the printer's 2016 note (Printer
# from Hell). Routed from the Janitor once printer_quest_done is set (#20).
scene janitor_dave
  if dave_janitor_done -> n6
  Mysterious Janitor: Dave? Dave Kowalski. Good man. Better IT specialist.
  Mysterious Janitor: He found something in the servers in 2016. Same thing Alex found years later. The shadow accounts.
  Mysterious Janitor: He reported it. Through official channels. By the book. And the book ate him alive.
  Mysterious Janitor: They transferred him to a branch that doesn't exist anymore. Closed six months later. Convenient timing.
  set dave_janitor_done

  @n6
  end

# (janitor_legacy_token deleted — an orphaned remnant of an earlier Legacy
# Admin design. It contradicted current canon twice over: the Phantom
# Approver quest attributes the bot to the previous IT manager, and the
# archive's 2006 admin_legacy request is signed by the Regional Manager.
# No NPC, interactable, or routing ever referenced it. Logic-sweep #20.)
# Signal booster interactables for Network Ghost quest
scene network_booster_br
  if booster_br_placed -> n4
  if network_started else -> n5
  Narrator: You press Alex's signal booster against the east wall. It clicks into place and a tiny green LED begins blinking.
  set booster_br_placed
  goto n6

  @n4
  Narrator: Signal booster: active. Green light steady.
  goto n6

  @n5
  Narrator: A small black device with an adhesive backing. You're not sure what it's for.

  @n6
  end

scene network_booster_stairwell
  if booster_stair_placed -> n4
  if network_started else -> n5
  Narrator: The concrete wall of the back corridor holds the signal booster perfectly. The LED blinks once, then settles into a steady green.
  set booster_stair_placed
  goto n6

  @n4
  Narrator: Signal booster: active. Green light steady.
  goto n6

  @n5
  Narrator: A small black device stuck to the wall. Looks like something an IT person would hide here.

  @n6
  end

scene network_booster_conf
  if booster_conf_placed -> n4
  if network_started else -> n5
  Narrator: The conference room's east wall. The signal booster fits neatly between two whiteboards. Nobody will notice it for months.
  set booster_conf_placed
  goto n6

  @n4
  Narrator: Signal booster: active. Green light steady.
  goto n6

  @n5
  Narrator: A small black device with an adhesive backing. You're not sure what it's for.

  @n6
  end

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUBQUEST 5: Printer's Soul
# Reward: Notarized Strike ability
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene alex_it_quest_printer
  if quest_printer_soul_complete -> n14
  if printer_soul_done -> n9
  if printer_soul_started -> n17
  Alex from IT: Okay so the printer thing. Not the haunted messages — I've been watching something else.
  Alex from IT: The printer draws about six times more power than it should in standby. It's not just idling. It's running a continuous background calculation. Has been for years.
  Alex from IT: I need the firmware disk to connect to it directly — should be on the equipment shelf in here. Then there's an ethernet port on the wall next to the printer. Plug in and I can pull the computation logs.
  set printer_soul_started
  goto n13

  @n7
  Alex from IT: Find the firmware disk on the equipment shelf first. Then the ethernet port is on the wall right next to the printer.
  goto n13

  @n8
  quest printers_soul stage 2
  goto n13

  @n9
  Alex from IT: I analyzed the computation logs. The printer has been generating the mathematical foundation for an unforgeable elliptic curve digital signature. For twenty-two years.
  Alex from IT: It didn't know what it was building. But I do. It's done now — it completed the sequence at the moment you connected.
  Alex from IT: I wrote you an attack from it. Notarized Strike. Carries the weight of twenty-two years of mathematical work. Very difficult to argue with.
  set quest_printer_soul_complete
  goto n15

  @n13
  end

  @n14
  Alex from IT: The printer finally went quiet. Twenty-two years of work — complete.
  goto n13

  @n15
  xp 350
  Narrator: Quest complete: Printer's Soul. +350 XP. Learned ability: Notarized Strike! A legally binding attack — Power 60.
  goto n13
  # Stage-aware reminder: once the disk is found, the stage-2 quest_update
  # (node 8) fires — it was orphaned before this gate (logic-sweep #20).

  @n17
  if printer_firmware_found else -> n7
  Alex from IT: You have the disk. Good. Now the ethernet port on the wall beside the printer — plug in, and the printer and I will handle the rest.
  goto n8

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUBQUEST 6: The Unauthorized Patch
# Reward: Invoke Charter ability
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene alex_it_quest_final
  if quest_final_patch_complete -> n17
  if patch_monitor_silenced -> n8
  if final_patch_started -> n6
  Alex from IT: I'm doing it. Nine months I've been waiting for an approved maintenance window. No one will sign off. The vulnerability is documented. The patch is written. Nobody cares.
  Alex from IT: It's a remote code execution flaw in the building's network OS. Nothing to do with trust accounts — just old, unpatched infrastructure that's been sitting here since 2019.
  Alex from IT: Building's empty. I'm deploying it tonight. But first — the network monitoring terminal near rack row three. It'll ping corporate the moment I start. Silence it, then come back.

  @n6
  set final_patch_started
  Alex from IT: The monitoring terminal is near rack row three. Kill the process, then come back and we'll deploy.
  goto n16

  @n8
  Alex from IT: Monitor's offline. Perfect. Starting the patch deploy now. Four minutes. And — there it is.
  Alex from IT: The building runs automated security sweeps when it detects unauthorized server access. I probably should have mentioned that. It won't stop the patch. It will, however, stop us.
  Alex from IT: I need you to handle the sweep while I finish the deploy. I have very strong feelings about not doing that myself.
  Narrator: Alex turns back to his keyboard and begins typing at an alarming speed. From the server room entrance, you hear footsteps.
  Security Guard: UNAUTHORIZED SERVER ACCESS DETECTED. PLEASE STEP AWAY FROM THE EQUIPMENT.
  Andrew: There is a completely legitimate explanation for this.
  Security Guard: SIR. STEP AWAY FROM THE EQUIPMENT.
  fight patch_defense

  @n16
  end

  @n17
  Alex from IT: Patch is live. Permanently encrypted into the network stack. They'd have to take down the whole building to roll it back.
  goto n16

scene patch_defense_defeated
  Narrator: The security sweep is handled. From behind three monitors, Alex raises one fist without looking up.
  Alex from IT: DONE. Patch deployed. Signed, distributed, live. It is permanently part of this building's infrastructure.
  Alex from IT: I wrote you an ability based on what I did tonight. Unauthorized, technically. Absolutely necessary. I'm calling it Invoke Charter. Don't ask why. It just felt right.
  set quest_final_patch_complete
  xp 400
  Narrator: Quest complete: The Unauthorized Patch. +400 XP. Learned ability: Invoke Charter! Power 100. Devastating to bad-faith enemies.
  end

# ==========================================================================
# PUZZLES
# ==========================================================================
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMPLIANCE CROSSWORD — 5 regulation questions for archive terminal password
# Talk to Compliance Auditor NPC on executive floor when act >= 3
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene compliance_crossword
  mode quiz
  if compliance_crossword_done -> n35
  Compliance Auditor: You again. I've been expecting you.
  Compliance Auditor: You want access to the secure terminal in the Archive. I know because I monitor all access requests. Even the ones that haven't been made yet.
  Compliance Auditor: The password is regulatory knowledge. Prove to me that you understand compliance, and I'll give you the key.
  Compliance Auditor: Five questions. Five correct answers. No partial credit. This is compliance, not a poetry class.
  @n5
  ask Compliance Auditor: Question 1: Under the prudent investor rule, a fiduciary must invest trust assets as what?
    -> n6 sets crossword_1=true: A reasonable person would.
    -> n8: A maximum-return optimizer.
    -> n8: The beneficiary requests.

  @n6
  Compliance Auditor: Correct. A reasonable person standard. Not a genius. Not Skip. A REASONABLE person.
  Compliance Auditor: Moving on.

  @n8
  ask Compliance Auditor: Question 2: What fiduciary duty prohibits self-dealing in trust administration?
    -> n9 sets crossword_2=true: Duty of loyalty.
    -> n11: Duty of care.
    -> n11: Duty of synergy.

  @n9
  Compliance Auditor: Correct. Loyalty. Not to be confused with the thing Skip asks for at every team meeting.
  Compliance Auditor: Three more.

  @n11
  ask Compliance Auditor: Question 3: What form must be filed with FINRA when a trust officer discovers a regulatory violation?
    -> n14: Form U4.
    -> n12 sets crossword_3=true: Form 27B/6.
    -> n14: Form 1099.

  @n12
  Compliance Auditor: Correct. Form 27B/6. Forty-seven pages. Double-sided. My personal favorite form.
  Compliance Auditor: You're doing well. Suspiciously well.

  @n14
  ask Compliance Auditor: Question 4: What is the maximum statute of limitations for breach of fiduciary duty in most jurisdictions?
    -> n17: 3 years.
    -> n15 sets crossword_4=true: 6 years.
    -> n17: There is no limit.

  @n15
  Compliance Auditor: Correct. Six years in most jurisdictions. Which means the admin_legacy account's activities are still within the window.
  Compliance Auditor: One more question.

  @n17
  ask Compliance Auditor: Final question: What document establishes the foundational obligations of a trust institution?
    -> n20: The operating agreement.
    -> n18 sets crossword_5=true: The trust charter.
    -> n20: The employee handbook.

  @n18
  Compliance Auditor: Correct. The trust charter. The very foundation this building stands on.
  Compliance Auditor: Interesting that you know that.

  @n20
  Compliance Auditor: Results are in.
  if crossword_1 else -> n30
  if crossword_2 else -> n30
  if crossword_3 else -> n30
  if crossword_4 else -> n30
  if crossword_5 else -> n30
  Compliance Auditor: Perfect score. I'm... impressed. That's the second time I've ever said that. The first was in a mirror.
  Compliance Auditor: The archive terminal password is: FIDUCIARY. All caps. No spaces. Because compliance doesn't believe in spaces.
  set compliance_crossword_done
  set has_archive_password
  goto n37

  @n30
  Compliance Auditor: Insufficient. Your regulatory knowledge has gaps. Like your department's internal controls.
  Compliance Auditor: Come back when you've studied. I recommend reading the entire CFR Title 12. It's only 40,000 pages.
  set crossword_1 = false
  set crossword_2 = false
  set crossword_3 = false

  @n35
  set crossword_4 = false
  set crossword_5 = false

  @n37
  end

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JANITOR'S RIDDLES — 3 visits, 3 riddles, +2 all stats
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene janitor_riddle_1
  mode quiz
  if riddle_1_attempted -> n3
  set riddle_1_attempted
  Mysterious Janitor: Ah, Andrew. Looking for wisdom? I have a riddle for you.

  @n3
  Mysterious Janitor: I am owed by many but owned by none. I am earned by actions, not by transactions. What am I?
  @n4
  ask Mysterious Janitor: Take your time.
    -> n5: Trust.
    -> n8: Money.
    -> n8: Respect.

  @n5
  Mysterious Janitor: Trust. Correct. The very thing this building was built to protect.
  Mysterious Janitor: Come back — I have more riddles.
  set janitor_riddle_1_done
  goto n10

  @n8
  Mysterious Janitor: Not quite. Think about what this place is supposed to protect. What people give us when they walk through those doors.
  Mysterious Janitor: Come back when you've thought about it.

  @n10
  end

scene janitor_riddle_2
  mode quiz
  if riddle_2_attempted -> n3
  set riddle_2_attempted
  Mysterious Janitor: Back for more? Good. Second riddle.

  @n3
  Mysterious Janitor: I grow stronger when tested, weaker when assumed. I am the foundation of every contract but written in no clause. What am I?
  @n4
  ask Mysterious Janitor: Think carefully.
    -> n5: Good faith.
    -> n8: Power.
    -> n8: Obligation.

  @n5
  Mysterious Janitor: Good faith. The implied covenant. Every contract assumes it, but no one can define it.
  Mysterious Janitor: Two for two. One more to go.
  set janitor_riddle_2_done
  goto n10

  @n8
  Mysterious Janitor: Close, but no. Think about what holds a contract together even when the words fail.
  Mysterious Janitor: Come back and try again.

  @n10
  end

scene janitor_riddle_3
  mode quiz
  if riddle_3_attempted -> n3
  set riddle_3_attempted
  Mysterious Janitor: The final riddle. The hardest one.

  @n3
  Mysterious Janitor: I was here before the building. I will be here after it falls. I am not in the charter, but the charter is in me. What am I?
  @n4
  ask Mysterious Janitor: Last chance.
    -> n5: Duty.
    -> n9: The building.
    -> n9: Memory.

  @n5
  Mysterious Janitor: Duty. Not the legal kind. The human kind. The obligation we feel to each other, written in no law but felt in every bone.
  Mysterious Janitor: Three for three. You remind me of someone I used to be.
  Narrator: The Janitor's Rolex glows briefly. You feel stronger. Wiser. More... composed.
  set janitor_riddle_3_done
  goto n11

  @n9
  Mysterious Janitor: Not quite. Duty predates every institution. It's what makes us build them in the first place.
  Mysterious Janitor: Come back when you've considered what brought you to this building.

  @n11
  end

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SOCIAL ENGINEERING — 3-step info chain from Isaiah → Diane → Intern
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene social_engineering_1
  if social_eng_started -> n6
  Isaiah: Hey Andrew. I overheard something. Meredith has an assistant — someone she brought with her. They're on the executive floor.
  Isaiah: The assistant has Meredith's schedule. If we knew when she was meeting the board, we could prepare.
  Isaiah: But the assistant won't talk to anyone from our department. We'd need to... get creative.
  Isaiah: Diane might know who the assistant is. She processes everyone who enters the building.
  set social_eng_started
  goto n7

  @n6
  Isaiah: Talk to Diane about Meredith's assistant. She processes everyone who enters the building.

  @n7
  end

scene social_engineering_2
  if social_eng_started else -> n7
  if social_eng_diane -> n7
  Diane: Meredith's assistant? Yes, I signed them in. Name is Morgan. Very young. Very nervous.
  Diane: They asked me where the bathroom was three times. And where to get coffee. They're out of their depth.
  Diane: If someone brought them a coffee — large, oat milk, extra shot — they might be grateful enough to chat.
  Diane: The Intern makes coffee runs. He could deliver it without raising suspicion.
  set social_eng_diane

  @n7
  end

scene social_engineering_3
  if social_eng_diane else -> n8
  if social_eng_complete -> n8
  The Intern: A coffee mission? A STEALTH coffee mission? This is the greatest day of my unpaid career!
  Narrator: Ten minutes later, the Intern returns, vibrating with excitement.
  The Intern: OKAY so Morgan is SUPER stressed. Meredith makes them carry three tablets and a backup clipboard.
  The Intern: The board meeting is scheduled for 4 PM tomorrow. Meredith is presenting a 'dissolution recommendation.' And she has backup from the Regional Manager's office.
  The Intern: Also Morgan said thank you for the coffee and asked if we're all going to be fired. I said 'probably not!' Which I realize was not reassuring.
  set social_eng_complete

  @n8
  end
